canonical_claim.statement:
Printed specification refuted (p. 61): 'We used the logistic regression model, $P(Y_{ij}=1)=1-\left(1+\exp\left(\alpha+\tau t_j+\beta n_j+\phi n_jt_j+\sum_{f=1}^{F-1}\gamma_f\Gamma_f\right)\right)^{-1}$ where $t_j$ is a treatment indicator for cluster $j$, $n_j$ is the cluster size, $F$ is the number of blocks (in this case, 40), $\Gamma_f$ is an indicator variable indicating whether cluster $j$ is in block $f$. The terms $\alpha$, $\tau$, $\beta$, $\phi$ and $\gamma_f$ are coefficients estimated from the data using maximum likelihood methods. Note the coefficient $\phi$ is responsible for the heterogeneous treatment effects. We estimate $\tau$ as 0.4, $\beta$ as 1.4 and $\phi$ as $-0.9$. We used this model to impute missing potential outcomes for each individual. To do so, the latent probability of response (voting) was first computed for each unit when treated, $p_{ti}$, and when not treated, $p_{ci}$, using the estimated model. ... Marginalizing over the imputation process, the ATE is in expectation $\frac{\sum_i(p_{ti}-p_{ci})}{N}=0.007$, or 0.7 percentage points.' Here $n_j$ is 'the number of individuals in the $j^{th}$ cluster' (p. 44).

Refutation (validator; a universal impossibility, compiled in Lean 4). Let $L(x)=1-(1+e^x)^{-1}$. Let $i=1,\ldots,N$ with $N\geq1$ be any finite nonempty population, let $\eta_{ci}$ be any real numbers (the control linear predictor $\alpha+\beta n_{j(i)}+\sum_f\gamma_f\Gamma_f$; no restriction on $\alpha$, $\beta$ or the block coefficients), and let the cluster size of $i$'s cluster satisfy $n_{j(i)}\geq1$. Put $p_{ci}=L(\eta_{ci})$ and $p_{ti}=L\left(\eta_{ci}+\frac25-\frac9{10}n_{j(i)}\right)$. Then:
(i) $L$ is strictly increasing;
(ii) $\frac25-\frac9{10}n\leq-\frac12$ for every real $n\geq1$;
(iii) $p_{ti}-p_{ci}<0$ for every $i$;
(iv) $\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N(p_{ti}-p_{ci})<0$, hence $\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N(p_{ti}-p_{ci})\neq\frac7{1000}$.
So no data are consistent at once with the printed model, the printed coefficients $\tau=0.4$, $\phi=-0.9$, the count definition of $n_j$, and the printed value $0.007$. Concrete instance: $N=1$, $n=1$, $\eta_c=0$ gives $p_c=\frac12$ and $p_t=L(-\frac12)<\frac12$.

Corrected specification (the validator's smallest repair; conditional on the size predictor having been measured in thousands of persons, which the validator calls plausible but not recovered from the publication): define $s_j=n_j/1000$ and replace $n_j$ by $s_j$ in the logistic display only,
$P(Y_{ij}=1)=1-\left(1+\exp\left(\alpha+\tau t_j+\beta s_j+\phi s_jt_j+\sum_{f=1}^{F-1}\gamma_f\Gamma_f\right)\right)^{-1}$, with $\tau=0.4$, $\beta=1.4$, $\phi=-0.9$;
equivalently, keep the raw count $n_j$ and report the size slopes as $0.0014$ and $-0.0009$.

Formal content, with every hypothesis. Let $L(x)=1-(1+e^x)^{-1}$; for a finite nonempty population $i=1,\ldots,N$, arbitrary real control linear predictors $\eta_{ci}$ and integer cluster sizes $n_{j(i)}\geq1$, put $p_{ci}=L(\eta_{ci})$ and $p_{ti}=L\left(\eta_{ci}+\frac25-\frac9{10000}n_{j(i)}\right)$. Then:
(i) the treatment-minus-control log-odds contrast $\frac25-\frac9{10000}n_j$ is $>0$ if and only if $n_j<\frac{4000}9$, that is, if and only if $n_j\leq444$, and is $<0$ if and only if $n_j\geq445$;
(ii) $p_{ti}>p_{ci}$ for every individual in a cluster with $n_j\leq444$, and $p_{ti}<p_{ci}$ for every individual in a cluster with $n_j\geq445$;
(iii) consequently the sign of $\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N(p_{ti}-p_{ci})$ is not forced: it is strictly positive whenever every cluster has $n_j\leq444$ and strictly negative whenever every cluster has $n_j\geq445$.
Unlike the printed specification, the corrected one therefore does not force the average to be negative; whether it equals the reported $0.007$ on the authors' sample is empirical and is not part of this target.
